<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annaba</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bône</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Annaba is a port city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, near the ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Saint Augustine</a>. Known in medieval and early modern times as Bona or Bône, it was a target of European raids, including a sack by the Tuscan Knights of Saint Stephen in 1607. It became a French colonial port after 1832 and is now one of Algeria's largest cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annaba.jpg" alt="Annaba" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dan Sloan · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annaba-building02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Annaba lies in the north-eastern corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, close to the Tunisian border and near the small Seybouse River. The present city grew up on the site of Aphrodisium, the seaport of the Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, and has since spread south over Hippo's ruins. Its former names Bône and Bona derive from Ubbo, a local form of Hippo. The Arabic name means &quot;place of the jujubes&quot;, after the abundance of that fruit in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> entered the record at the end of the 3rd century BCE as a possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Massinissa</a>'s Numidian kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> was its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> from 396 until his death in 430. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> destroyed the city in the 5th century and ruled it for about a century; in 534 their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a> surrendered at Bône to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>, general of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-the-maghreb">Muslim conquest of the Maghreb</a> in 699 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and Zirids ruled it in turn. Flooding and the ravages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> in 1033 led to its relocation to its present site.</p><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> conquered it in 1153 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> took it in 1160. Hafsid rule began in 1250, and Ottoman rule in 1533, apart from Spanish rule from 1535 to 1540.</p><p>Under French rule from 1832, as Bône, it was a main French settlement with a sizeable pied-noir minority. A sheltered port of 80 hectares was built between 1856 and 1869 for iron ore from the Mokta el Hadid mine, and the railway from the mine, opened in 1864, was the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. The mine produced 22,000 tonnes in 1865 and 255,000 in 1869. Bône had 10,000 inhabitants before the mine and 41,000 by 1924. In 1943 it was a crucial road and sea base for the Allied invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-torch">Operation Torch</a>. It remained part of French Algeria until independence in 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Annaba is a leading industrial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, with a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> complex at El Hadjar south of the city. The Basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Saint Augustine</a> recalls the ancient diocese of Hippo. The Bône War Cemetery, 5 kilometres from the city, holds 868 Commonwealth burials of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Annaba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Order of Saint Stephen in 1561, a crusading order of knights based at Pisa, whose galleys fought the Ottomans and the Barbary corsairs; Tuscan galleys served in the Christian fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571, and in 1607 the knights sacked <b>Bona</b> on the Algerian coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Military</a></li></ul></section>
